         (no mapped test)
         recommended test: Merging two duplicate candidate obligations of different types (e.g. a functional obligation and a test_demand obligation, or a functional and a boundary obligation) leaves them as two separate obligations; merging two of the same type is permitted.
           inputs:  In tests/requirement/test_linking.py, build a Decomposition of two ordinary (satisfied_by_absence=False) obligations of different types (e.g. 'alpha' FUNCTIONAL, 'beta' INVARIANT) via _typed_decomposition, with the earlier one first. Supply a prior-merge record naming exactly that pair as merged: a ledger entry / MergeDecision (use the ledger.LedgerEntry/MergeDecision types the linking module imports) that says alpha and beta are the same requirement. Also add a second case with three obligations: A and B same type (FUNCTIONAL) and C of a different type (INVARIANT), where prior verdicts or a transitive chain A~B and B~C would put all three into one component. Use a client that would confirm every pair it is asked about, so only the structural guard can stop the merge. Also include a control pair of the SAME type that has a prior merge decision, and a control mixed pair of two scope exclusions of different types with a prior decision.
           detects: The same-type rule is applied only where `_can_state_one_requirement` is called, when pairs are put to the model. A merge that arrives by another route, such as a recorded MergeDecision or ledger entry replayed from an earlier run, or a transitive component built from earlier verdicts, may never go through that guard. Two non-exclusion obligations of different types could then still merge, and one would be silently retyped.
           full detail: acceptance recommendation --criterion merge-non-scope-exclusion-same-type-only

