    [scope_too_narrow] scope-exclusion-bullets-become-absence-obligations/prompt-example-covers-only-aside-clause-shape
      The new decomposition prompt guidance covers a trailing 'why' clause and gives a single example of the 'does not alter' rewrite. Other bullet shapes may still be restated as work or as a property to hold. Examples are a bullet naming a feature to build later, a bullet phrased as 'X stays unchanged', and a bullet in the imperative. The old paragraph distinguishing work exclusions from behaviour exclusions was removed.
      src/acceptance/requirement/obligations.py#1
      pairs: 28 put to the model, 114 skipped once the defect was covered
    [missing_case] scope-exclusion-bullets-become-absence-obligations/exclusion-bullet-yields-no-obligation
      Exclusion status and the absence obligation depend on each bullet being parsed as an EXCLUSION requirement and then surviving decomposition into an obligation. A bullet that is not recognised, for example a nested bullet or a variant heading, would get no absence obligation. A bullet the model drops or merges away would also get none. Merging two exclusions keeps only the earlier obligation's description and gains the other's spans. A merge across distinct bullets could therefore leave one exclusion without its own absence statement.
      src/acceptance/requirement/obligations.py#2
      src/acceptance/requirement/obligations.py#3
      src/acceptance/requirement/linking.py#2
      pairs: 5 put to the model, 137 skipped once the defect was covered

  scope-exclusion-with-explanatory-clause-yields-absence-obligation  (reused from an earlier run)
    [qualifier_ignored] scope-exclusion-with-explanatory-clause-yields-absence-obligation/aside-still-restated-as-work-to-do
      The only mechanism that keeps the obligation in the negative form is prose in the decomposition prompt. No code enforces it. The model can still turn the bullet "X, which is not part of a review run" into "The change excludes X from the review run", which treats the aside as the operative instruction and restates the excluded work as work to do.
      src/acceptance/requirement/obligations.py#1
      pairs: 4 put to the model, 138 skipped once the defect was covered
    [other] scope-exclusion-with-explanatory-clause-yields-absence-obligation/aside-yields-extra-obligation
      The explanatory clause ("which is not part of a review run") could be decomposed into a second obligation of its own next to the absence obligation. The criterion requires exactly one obligation per bullet, and no code deduplicates or caps the obligations from one exclusion bullet. Each would also be forced to code_only and satisfied_by_absence.
      src/acceptance/requirement/obligations.py#1
      src/acceptance/requirement/obligations.py#2
      pairs: 4 put to the model, 138 skipped once the defect was covered
    [missing_case] scope-exclusion-with-explanatory-clause-yields-absence-obligation/marker-without-bullet-text-when-spans-empty
      The marker quotes the bullet only if the obligation has non-empty source_spans. If spans are empty, or cover only part of the bullet and so omit the exclusion text, the block renders a bare "(scope exclusion)" or a truncated quote. A mis-worded description such as "excludes X from Y" then has no boundary text to be judged against, and the coverage stage can still report it not addressed because the excluded work is absent.
      src/acceptance/coverage/prompt.py#0
      src/acceptance/coverage/classify.py#0
